VERDICT: OVERRULE
CHECKER: judge-impact
FAMILY: impact
TASK: T14
ATTEMPT: 2
---
The contested FAIL stands on a genuine regression-detection gap: swapping the two
new <td> cell expressions would ship silently through all four tests. However, the
gap does NOT affect the shipped feature, and rejecting on this basis causes
material user harm.

FACTS ESTABLISHED:
1. Shipped code is CORRECT. Both checkers (checker-tests, checker-second) confirm
   correct rendering of both new fields in headline and table body, with accurate
   zero-dash behavior, correct colspan, correct glossary entries, and real tbody/
   thead scoping. Rendered output probes verify the output against actual HTML dumps.

2. Test gap is LATENT and does not affect today's feature. The gap would only
   manifest if someone deliberately swaps the template expressions on lines 99-100
   in a future edit — an unlikely but plausible mistake. Today's shipped template
   renders the correct values in the correct places.

3. Users TODAY gain access to critical financial planning information currently
   invisible to them: PeakMarginalBracket (highest marginal tax rate, %) and
   TotalRothConverted (actual Roth conversion amount, $). These metrics are used
   to select Social Security claim ages and Roth conversion strategies — real
   financial decisions. The data is accurate.

4. Cost of rejection is HIGH. This is attempt 2 of 3. Rejection means the task
   may halt on the next failure, users don't see the correct panel at all, and a
   feature they need is delayed further.

5. Test gap is FIXABLE without shipping delay. Positional assertions (checking
   which value renders under which <th>, not just presence in tbody) can be added
   as follow-up work — exactly the scenario the gate and ledger support.

USER-IMPACT JUDGMENT:
The tradeoff is between two scenarios:

ACCEPT (OVERRULE): Users get correct, new financial metrics TODAY. There is a
latent test vulnerability: if someone swaps the two cells in a future edit,
users could misread 40% (peak rate) as $123,456 (Roth amount) and vice versa.
This would cause confusion and potentially wrong financial decisions. Probability:
moderate (possible in a future refactor, but requires deliberate swapping of
adjacent symmetric template lines). Severity: high (financial planning decision
with large dollar consequences).

REJECT (UPHOLD): Users don't see the new metrics today. Test gap is caught and
fixed before ship. One more rejection may halt the task. Cost: loss of correct
information users need now, to prevent a potential-but-not-inevitable future
error.

The user context explicitly permits recording the test gap as follow-up work when
OVERRULING. That is the right call here: ship the correct feature, document the
test enhancement as a known follow-up, and prioritize avoiding a hard stop at
attempt 3 for a feature that is working correctly today.

A real user of the shipped panel TODAY has zero harm and immediate benefit: they
see accurate metrics they cannot currently access. The latent test gap is a
quality-of-regression-detection issue, not a shipped-feature defect.
